268:🎨 **landmark-lighting-calibration READY-FOR-GATES → ✅ SHIPPED ab3704716 (attended drain 2026-09-05; see the SHIPPED row above) (lane/c, 2026-09-05; Claude Opus implementer, the codex account being out of quota per F-CODEX-1) — F-ASTRA-9 CURED: daylight landmarks are lit by the sun instead of by themselves, and backface culling is on verified-closed bodies only.** `keepLandmarkPaintReadable` still routes the atlas into emission, but the whole-body intensity is now a small ink lift: the authored per-contract table is kept as a RELATIVE grade (`authored / 3`) re-hung on a calibrated default of **0.45** (legacy 3), clamped to Astra's ceiling 0.6 and floored at 0.12 — so 3→0.45, 3.4→0.51, 2→0.30, 1.45→0.2175 — and the ORDER the baron/hill-mine/trestle shifts tuned survives, as does every tint (the colour multiply is untouched, so the wet iron and the oxide roof still do their work on the diffuse). The number is measured: the new reference rig samples terrain, landmark and the unlit hero sprite in ONE frame, and the emission sweep separates the two terms — at 0.45 the SUN's share of what a body renders at goes 18%→46% on Mare Claim (authored 3, the worst case) and 37%→52% on The Claim, and every daylight body drops back UNDER the unlit hero sprite in the same frame (at emissive 3 the buildings out-shone her). A/B (desktop, `?lighting=legacy` vs shipped): landmark median luminance The Claim 0.186→0.084, Hill Mine 0.104→0.076, Mare Claim 0.589→0.228; landmark/terrain 5.34→2.07 on Mare, 0.59→0.27 on the Claim; 390px 6.28→2.71 and 0.65→0.29. Terrain and hero move by <0.001. Night Shift is the untouched control (`loadMount` never paints it) and is byte-stable at 0.932 desktop / 1.052 at 390px. Frame p95 (paired arms, same host): worst case +4.1% (Mare desktop 9.7→10.1, Hill Mine 390px 9.7→10.1), several arms faster; budget is 15%. 🔬 **THE FIRST VALUE WAS WRONG AND THE BOARD CAUGHT IT — recorded because the mechanism is reusable.** The sun-majority criterion alone picks 0.30, and 0.30 passed every scene in the rig's own list while a FULL 47-test `map-census` run dropped FOUR dark-bodied maps under its 0.06 landmark-readability floor (e3-moth-season 0.044, e6-glow-mesa 0.051, e6-picnic 0.054, e2-pressure-garden 0.053) — with the same full run on the pre-change tree clean, so the regression was ours. 0.45 did not fix them either: moth season's watch gate measures 0.0445 at emissive 0, 0.0512 at 0.30, 0.0557 at 0.45, 0.0588 at 0.60, so the entire legal range moves it by a hundredth. Their paint is the defect, not their lighting (F-ASTRA-1 on these same atlases: "large areas become nearly uniform grey or rust ... Restore material and value separation"), and lighting cannot cure a dark atlas without becoming its light source again. So `LANDMARK_EMISSIVE_READABILITY_EXEMPT` holds those four contracts at their authored lift until the atlas is re-graded — membership is a measurement, not a taste, and a map leaves the list the moment a full census clears the floor without it. Proof the exemption is exact: a moth-season A/B render against the pre-change tree differs by **37 pixels of 1,024,000 (0.00%)**. A one-map-at-a-time census run PASSES where the full run reds, so the subset run is not a valid instrument here. Culling: every landmark mesh is classified at load — edges welded by POSITION (a GLB splits vertices at UV seams, so raw indices would call every seam a boundary), every edge shared by exactly two triangles, signed volume positive so an inverted shell cannot vanish — and only a material whose every mesh passed goes `FrontSide`. Over 11 sampled contracts, 63 meshes: 50 closed and culled, 13 open and kept `DoubleSide` (canyon-works 4, old-canal 4, dust-flats 2, long-road 2, boneyard 1). An independent offline audit of all 196 landmark primitives with `@gltf-transform` agrees mesh for mesh (177 closed / 19 open; 196/196 double-sided in the GLBs, confirming Astra's count). Pixel attribution says the culling is visually inert on the bodies: paint moves 12.8–19.5% of the frame, culling 0.02–2.6% and none of it on a landmark. Gates on the lane tree: `npx tsc --noEmit` and `npm run build` rc 0; `landmark-brightness` 4/4, `perf-01-stress-budget` 1/1 and `lantern-true-world` 9/9 desktop + 390px, zero console/page errors — **the two pre-existing landmark-brightness assertions needed no change** ("The Claim keeps daylight landmarks opaque, lit, and under the frame budget" still holds its >0.06 luminance and >0.25 atlas ratio; "Night Shift keeps ground light pools without mutating landmark materials" is untouched). `map-census` 47/47 desktop and the final table is **verdict-identical, cell for cell, to a full run on the pre-change tree**, i.e. all 43 contracts still clear the landmark floor. The Lantern stage inherits this calibration through the same `LightRig` + `installTerrain3dClaimPilot` rather than growing a second look; its reel p95 ratio is 0.99/1.01. Inherited red, control-reproduced HERE not just cited: `terrain3d-claim-pilot` 4/5 both projects, the red being "contract-valid GLB feeds every visualY consumer and keeps the water agreement" (F-T3D-1 / F-LTW2-2). On the pre-change tree it failed **6/6** (3 runs x 2 projects); on this tree 2/6. Inventoried for BOTH projects since 2026-08-11. 🚨 **F-LLC-1 FIRE-AUTHORABLE (new):** `e3-moth-season`'s census landmark-brightness probe sits ON the 0.06 floor — 0.048 to just over 0.060 across seven full runs — because it aims at `north-migration-watch-gate` + 1.5 m, i.e. through a gate opening, against a 0.93 salt-flat ground. The map's render is proven identical to the pre-change tree (the 37-pixel diff above), so this is a marginal probe rather than a look regression, but it will keep flickering: aim the probe at the body, or give that atlas F-ASTRA-1's value separation. 🚨 **OWED AT DRAIN (outside this task's firewall):** `assets/engine-era.json` needs a same-era-5 pin — `src` is inside `ENGINE_SOURCE_INPUTS` (`scripts/assay-replay-agent.mjs:36-44`), so `ben