950:🚪 **F-2310-1 — THE FIFTH AXIS IS THE BROWSER ITSELF: `fetch(keepalive: true)` CAPS THE PLAYER'S SUBMISSION AT ~64 KiB, SO NO SERVER-SIDE ENVELOPE LAW CAN EVER ADMIT A BARON EPIC (fire s2310 2026-08-25).** ⚖️ **`door-epic-envelope-v2` STOPPED LAWFULLY — a FIREWALL SUCCESS, and exactly the outcome s2309 predicted in its own handoff (*"if a FIFTH axis emerges it will STOP, and that is a success to re-author, not a failure"*).** The runner's report opens **`STOP — not READY-FOR-GATES, and no commit was made`**, and it stopped for the right reason, in its own words: *"`Game.ts` is explicitly outside this task's touch-only list, so I stopped instead of widening scope."* ⚠️ **A DIFF NEVERTHELESS EXISTS AND IS NOT A DRAIN SIGNAL — THE SAME TRAP F-2308-1 NAMED ONE FIRE AGO, AND IT CAUGHT THE SAME LINEAGE TWICE: the lane RUNNER auto-commits, so the six authorized-but-unfinished files were swept into `lane/c` as `5645c5b91` (6 files, +198/−67) by machinery, not by a finished slice.** `lane-usable` reads `ahead=1 paths=6` and `drain-block-check` read `✅ CLEAR` — **both signals TRUE, neither a verdict** (Mistake #13 read in the other direction). The verdict lives in the runner's report, and it says STOP. 📊 **THE MEASUREMENT, taken by the runner and VERIFIED AT SOURCE by this fire rather than inherited (Mistake #4): `src/game/Game.ts` submits standings with `keepalive: true` — the flag re-read on main at `Game.ts:7244`, inside the fetch options of the submit call the runner cites at `:7221`. Chromium ACCEPTED a 60,000-byte body and REJECTED both 65,536 bytes and the compact 463,569-byte Baron body with `TypeError: Failed to fetch` — the request never reaches the ledger server at all.** 🔑 **WHY THIS IS THE DECISIVE ONE OF THE FIVE, AND WHY IT IS DIFFERENT IN KIND FROM ITS FOUR PREDECESSORS: duration (cured v2), bytes (`MAX_TAPE_BYTES`), entries (`MAX_PLAYBOOK_INTENTS`) and the L1 reader cap (`serve.mjs`) are all OUR constants — every one of them is a number the county chose and the county can change. This one is not ours.** ~64 KiB is the browser's own keepalive quota, enforced client-side before a byte leaves the tab, and it is invisible to every server-side test the factory runs: **`test-standings.mjs` and the ledger worker both exercise the HTTP surface DIRECTLY, so the whole envelope can be green end-to-end on the server while the real player path silently cannot post.** That is why four consecutive masters, each correctly scoped to the wall in front of it, kept finding another one behind it. 🔺 **THIS REPRICES OPEN DESK ITEM F-2299-2 RATHER THAN ADDING A NEW ONE — the desk stays at 38 DELIBERATELY, because this is new EVIDENCE on a question already sitting there, not a new question.** F-2299-2 reads *"Baron reel 464,285 B vs a 64 KiB ceiling; rec (b) keep 64 KiB"*, and F-2302-1 already warned that item's premise was the wrong ceiling. **Both are now sharpened by measurement: on the player path 64 KiB is not a policy preference the owner is choosing to keep — it is a browser-imposed constraint, so option (b) is very nearly the only option the current call site permits.** The two lawful ways out are now concrete and can be priced against each other: **(a)** admit the `Game.ts` call site and omit `keepalive` above the browser ceiling — the runner's own "smallest re-authored fix", which trades the unload-survival guarantee `keepalive` exists to provide, for the ability to post an epic; or **(b)** keep the ceiling and make Baron-class tapes fit, which is the direction F-2299-2 already recommends and which leaves the submit path untouched. 🚫 **NOT FIRE-AUTHORED, AND THE RESTRAINT IS THE POINT (CLAUDE.md §7.3): the runner handed over a fully-specified fix, and it was tempting to queue it.** But its premise is precisely the open fork — **under (b) the correct move is to shrink tapes and the `keepalive` edit is unwanted work on the player's submission path** — so authoring it would settle an owner design fork by implementing one side of it. A