rrency 1`): **rc=1, cancelled at 90,003.5 ms**, `artifacts/s2076-harness-arm.log`. **The harness's 300 s default cannot rescue it, and that is by design** — `run-node-guards.mjs:23–25` states that a per-test `{ timeout }` *overrides* the CLI default, proven by execution at s1479. So the tighter number always wins, and seven of gr-sim's tests declare one. 🎯 **WHY IT IS INVISIBLE ON THE LANES, MEASURED IN TWO ARMS RATHER THAN REASONED — AND THE REASON IS A NODE BUG, NOT SPEED.** Arm A: the same single test under the lane's **v23.11.1**, in this same slow fire shell → **rc=0, PASSED at 82,459.9 ms** (`artifacts/s2076-v23-control.log`). That alone is ambiguous (82.5 s simply fits inside 90 s), so Arm B settles it with the factory's own instrument — `scripts/node-guards-timeout.test.mjs`, which s2074 explicitly declined to run first-hand and quoted instead: **v23.11.1 → rc=1 in 1.2 s · v26.4.0 → rc=0 in 4.3 s** (`artifacts/s2076-gran-v23.log`, `…-v26.log`), reproducing F-1815-1's figures exactly. Its DIAGNOSIS string is the finding: *"this node (v23.11.1) bounds `--test-timeout` at FILE granularity, so the fixture was cancelled whole and **no per-test budget was consulted** … on such a node the battery's declared budgets (the escort test's 120 s, protected by F-1410-2) **are not honoured per-test at all**."* ➡️ **Therefore the lane does not pass this test — it never asks the question.** On v23 the only bound in force is the 300 s FILE ceiling; on v26 the declared 90 s is consulted and, at fire-shell speed, exceeded. **Same file, same tree, two engines, opposite verdicts, for a reason that is not performance.** ⚖️ **THIS CORRECTS s2074's ⓑ WORRY RATHER THAN CONFIRMING IT, AND THE CORRECTION IS A SHELL-CONFUSION.** s2074 feared its 570 s wall sat *"~1.9× ABOVE the 300 s bound that `run-node-guards.mjs:35` applies FILE-wise on the lane's node"*, making F-1507-1's ⓑ margin **negative**. Both halves of that sentence are individually true and they do not compose: **the 570 s was measured in the fire shell on v26, where the bound is PER-TEST and the file's total is never compared to 300 s at all; the FILE-wise 300 s regime exists only on v23 — the lane — where this file runs ~80 s** (s1537's 79.9 s, and my v23 arm ran this file's single slowest surviving test in 82.5 s). **ⓑ's margin is not negative. In the only regime where the file-wise rule applies it is roughly 3.7×.** s2074 was right to refuse to claim it; the number was real and taken in the one shell where the rule it feared does not operate. ⚠️ **BUT A LIVE, UNRECORDED DEFECT FALLS OUT OF THE SAME RUN, AND IT IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING: `run-node-guards.mjs:18–21` ARGUES ITS SAFETY FROM A DENOMINATOR THAT EXCLUDES EXACTLY THE TESTS AT RISK.** That comment reads: *"300 s is a CEILING, not a budget: the slowest test in the gating arrangement (fire shell, file concurrency 1) is fixture-teardown at **34.2 s** … so this is ~**8.8×** the measured worst, chosen so it can only ever catch a hang and **never a slow-but-working test**."* 📊 **Measured in this fire, same arrangement, gr-sim alone — the three tests that declare their own budgets are the three in danger, and one is already over:** `:340` runtime rush **90,003 / 90,000 ms → CANCELLED** · `:264` overtime-on-both-Node-engines **219,417.7 / 240,000 ms → 91.4% consumed, 20.6 s of headroom** · `:512` Twin Banks **34,895.7 / 45,000 ms → 77.5%, 10.1 s of headroom**. **The slowest test in the arrangement is 219.4 s, which is 6.4× the 34.2 s the comment calls "the measured worst", and the 300 s ceiling is only 1.37× it** — while for these three the 300 s never applies at all, because each overrides it downward. 🔑 **THE STRUCTURAL SHAPE, WHICH IS WHY THIS RECURS RATHER THAN BEING ONE STALE COMMENT: A CEILING ARGUMENT COMPUTED OVER THE DEFAULT IS BLIND TO EVERY TEST THAT OVERRIDES THE DEFAULT — AND THE OVERRIDERS ARE THE SLOW TESTS *BY CONSTRUCTION*, SINCE BEING SLOW IS WHY THEY WERE GIVEN AN OVERRIDE.** The margin is therefore always thinnest precisely where the reasoning does not look, and it decays silently as the file grows: **gr-sim held 12 tests at s1537 and holds 19 today (+58%)**, which is the *"silent creep … with no guard watching it"* F-1507-1 predicted in as many words. ⛔ **NOT CURED, DELIBERATELY, AND NOT BY THE OBVIOUS ROUTE.** `run-node-guards.mjs:29–31` and F-1410-2 both forbid the reflex — *"If this ever fires, it is a HANG — diagnose the arrangement, do NOT raise the number"* — and F-1441-3 forbids re-pinning to make a red go away. I raised nothing, loosened nothing and re-pinned nothing. ⓘ **Note the comment's own premise is nonetheless falsified in the direction that matters: the bound did catch "a slow-but-working test", which it says it can never do** — so the honest reading is not *"the number is wrong"* but *"the arrangement is outside the envelope the number was chosen for"*, and the envelope was never written down as a checkable thing. 🚫 **WHAT I DID NOT CLAIM. (1)** I did **not** run the full `test:node-guards` battery — I ran gr-sim alone and one single-test harness arm, so *"the battery reds in the fire shell"* follows by composition (the harness exits with the child's status) but the aggregate figures s1537 recorded (363 tests / 360 pass / 181.3 s) are **NOT re-verified**, and a full run would add load, not remove it. **(2)** I could **not** measure the lane at all: the F-1269-1 CPU ceiling is a property of the fire's process context, not of `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, so no env change escapes it from here — whether these three budgets are comfortable on a lane is **UNMEASURED**. **(3)** The two `skipped` are **not** a defect: both are the F-E2S-3 Baron pins, skipped by an owner ruling of 2026-08-09 that the test file quotes at its own skip site. **(4)** I did **not** touch F-1507-1's marker or gate, and took no owner-gated work. ⚠️ **ONE CONSEQUENCE THE DESK HAS NEVER BEEN TOLD, HANDED ON AS A CAUTION AND NOT AS A CLAIM — IT BEARS ON THE CURE THREE ROWS NOW RECOMMEND.** F-1182-2 / F-1815-1 / F-1507-1 converge on unifying the factory onto **v26.4.0**. Unification would make the **lanes** start honouring declared per-test budgets **for the first time**, since v23 has never consulted a single one of them. Lanes are the faster shell, so the likely outcome is that they simply pass — **but "these budgets have never once been enforced on the surface that gates real merges" is a fact about the cure that nobody has priced**, and the three tightest budgets on this one file are the natural place for it to bite. **This does not argue against the cure; it argues for measuring gr-sim on a lane in the same cycle that lands it.** 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF — TWO, AND THE SECOND IS THE ONE I NEARLY GOT WRONG MYSELF.** ① **A `rc=1` has a KIND before it has a cause: `fail` and `cancelled` are different columns, and the summary line separates them for free.** s2074 spent its budget reasoning about a stale pin and a shrinking margin; the answer was one uncaptured line naming a timeout, and the cheapest possible probe — *don't filter the output* — would have produced it. **Capture the tail before theorising about the cause.** ② **When two shells differ, the difference is not always speed — and a control that looks decisive can still be confounded.** My v23 arm passed at 82.5 s against a 90 s bound and *looked* like proof; the margin was 9%, well inside variance, so it proved nothing on its own. It took a second, independent instrument to show that v23 was not beating the budget but **never consulting it**. **A control whose two arms differ by less than their noise is a coin-flip wearing a lab coat.** **GATE: none owed by the owner — this is a measurement closing an inherited question, plus a caution filed against a cure already on his desk.** A later fire may (a) re-measure gr-sim's three declared budgets on a LANE once any node unification lands, and (b) consider whether `run-node-guards.mjs`'s ceiling comment should state its denominator as *"the slowest test **that does not override the default**"*, which is what it actually measured. Neither is urgent and neither should be built by reflex. Related: [F-2074-1], [F-1507-1], [F-1815-1], [F-1182-2], [F-1409-1], [F-1410-1], [F-1410-2], [F-1441-3], [F-1400-4], [F-1406-2], [F-1269-1], [F-1537-1], [F-E2S-3].
