rent table designs; and the asset paths are **brace-expansion shorthand** (`bld-{reactor-dome,isotope-kitchen,…}.png`) which matches no literal filename anywhere, by construction. Each control cost one probe, and together they moved the verdict from *"the art ledger holds lost history"* to *"nothing is lost"* — **a hit against a corpus must be controlled before it is believed to condemn, and a probe that reports a shorthand as a missing file will report EVERY such row as missing, every time it is run.** ⓘ **HONEST LIMIT: "dormant" is measured by mtime, which a copy operation would reset — but the totals matching s1501 to the byte independently corroborate that the set has not moved.** 📎 Also noted, cosmetic: F-1501-3's header says **553 MB** where its own body says **527.89 MB**; the body is the measured figure. 🪞 **AND IT BIT A THIRD TIME, INSIDE THIS FIRE'S OWN BOOKKEEPING, WHICH IS WHY IT IS WORTH A CLAUSE RATHER THAN A SHRUG: my splice keyed the F-1501-3 row with `includes('**F-1501-3')`, and the FIRST match was **F-1533-1** — a different row that merely CITES F-1501-3 in bold — so the premise correction landed on the wrong finding and was caught only because the reported line (581) disagreed with the 657 `desk-state-audit` had printed minutes earlier.** Stripped and re-applied by keying on the row SUBJECT (marker, then the id at the row's head) rather than on containment; verified 1 occurrence, F-1533-1 restored byte-clean. ⓘ **`desk-state-audit.mjs` already encodes exactly this rule — its classification is deliberately SUBJECT-FIRST, because an id merely cited inside another finding's subject zone carries no state — so the correct instrument existed and I hand-rolled a weaker one anyway.** ➡️ **A ONE-LINE-PER-ROW LEDGER MAKES EVERY CITATION LOOK LIKE A SUBJECT TO A `grep`-SHAPED MATCHER; when a coordinate from an instrument disagrees with the one your own matcher found, the INSTRUMENT is the control — stop and reconcile before writing.**
