nvokes `node --test` with `--test-timeout=0`", but the script has since moved to `scripts/run-node-guards.mjs` (F-1409-1) and passes **no** timeout flag at all. The defect survived the rewrite because **node injects its own `--test-timeout=0` when the flag is absent** — measured s1479 by reading the child's `execArgv` — so the row was describing the *effect* correctly while naming a command that no longer existed. **Presence of the flag proves nothing; the VALUE is the whole discriminator**, and the guard is written that way. 🚫 **NOTHING WAS RAISED — the distinction F-1410-2 cares about:** a per-test `{ timeout }` overrides the CLI default, so the escort test's protected **120 s** and Twin Banks' 45 s are untouched; the other 265 move from unbounded to bounded, which is strictly tighter. **300 s is a CEILING, not a budget** — the slowest test in the real gating arrangement (fire shell, file concurrency **1**) is fixture-teardown at **34.2 s**, then 15.1 s and 14.9 s, so the bound sits at ~8.8× the measured worst and can only ever catch a hang. Sizing it tight would trade an unbounded hang for a flaky red, and **a red nobody trusts is the more expensive failure** (F-1460-1: five fires ignored a red wearing an excused label). ✅ **PROVEN BY EXECUTING THE VIOLATION PATH, never by a green** (s1299/s1300 standard): `scripts/node-guards-timeout.test.mjs` reds on all three mutations — pre-cure state and `=0` both → *"must be finite and positive, got --test-timeout=0"*, and `=3_600_000` → *"no longer protects a fire's window"* — with `run-node-guards.mjs` restored **byte-identical** (sha256 `5e4635543af4`). The override direction is proven the same way: at `--test-timeout=1000` an unbounded test was **cancelled at 1003 ms** while a `{ timeout: 10_000 }` sibling **passed at 3003 ms**. Probe retained at `logs/session-scratch/s1479-test-timeout-semantics.probe.mjs`. 🩺 Guard rooted in `test:node-guards` (unrooted would red `gate-caller-audit`); battery **305 tests / 302 pass / 0