5249:- 🚀 **s1452 REFILL — lane-c DISPATCHED `f1452-1-fort-solidity-routes-long-static-blockers`, the F-BW-19 successor the last three handoffs named as the board's highest priority.** Leaf `f1452-1-fort-solidity-static-routing` registered `queued` in the master's own commit; `e1-baron-fort-solidity` stays **`stopped`** and `lane-baron-fort-solidity.md` is still **not to be re-queued unchanged** — its STOP was correct and this supersedes nothing. ⭐ **THE MECHANISM WAS RE-DERIVED BY READING THE CODE AT AUTHORING TIME RATHER THAN INHERITED FROM THE STOP'S PROSE, AND THE FIRST HYPOTHESIS WAS WRONG — recorded because the wrong one was plausible enough to have shipped.** The seam was initially placed at `BuildSystem.reservedFootprints`, on the strength of the collision getter admitting static bodies via `blocksRouting !== false`; reading `Game.ts` showed `reservedFootprints` is **megaprojects**, and that landmark colliders travel a *different* road entirely (`Terrain.landmarkBlockers()` ← `landmarkBlockersFor` ← the registry JSON). ✅ **The corrected, verified mechanism:** enemies **already** receive every registered landmark as a collider — `Game.ts` passes `[...this.buildSystem.palisadeBlockers, ...this.heroBlockers()]` and `heroBlockers()` spreads `Terrain.landmarkBlockers()` — and respond with local slide + depenetration in `resolveBlocker`; but enemy **routing** asks `BuildSystem.palisadeRoute`, which iterates `this.targets.palisade` **only**, as do all three of its private helpers. **A landmark is therefore structurally invisible to the route graph: a long fort wall is something an enemy can bump into but never plan around** — precisely the >1,400-fixed-tick stall the s1439 STOP measured, and the reason registry entries alone are a regression rather than a fix. The asymmetry sits one screen apart in the same file. ⚠️ **The constraint the master states up front so the runner cannot discover it the expensive way: `PalisadeRoute.blocker` is consumed as a wrecker GNAW TARGET** (`route.blocker` → `isBuildingValid` → HP chewing), while a `LandmarkBlocker` carries only id/x/z/halfX/halfZ with no `hp`, `family` or `position` — so a static body must yield a **waypoint without becoming gnawable**, and **whether enemies may ever damage a landmark fort is an OWNER/CANON question, explicitly OUT OF SCOPE and to be reported rather than decided.** The stall test must be proved load-bearing by a **manufactured red** (revert scope 1 → RED, restore → GREEN), since a green that passes both ways proves nothing about a stall. Dispatched in the **F-1424-3 order** — master + leaf + this row committed FIRST, lane refreshed SECOND, all **eight** citation keys proved `=1` on main **and again inside the refreshed lane** (F-1425-2), `cp` THIRD — and it carries the **FACTORY-CHURN EXCEPTION** (F-1407-1). **GATE: F-BW-19 closes when the owner can no longer walk through the fort walls AND an enemy crosses the same wall line without stalling, both measured.**
